INSERT INTO public.marketing_bible_versions (content_md, pillars, personas, change_summary, created_by)
SELECT $md$# Pedra Silva Arquitectos — Marketing Bible

## Purpose

This is the strategy every piece of Pedra Silva Arquitectos communication is measured against. It guides what we post, how we say it and who we say it to.

It is a living draft. Luis defines and approves it, informed by the senior team and the team questionnaire.

## Who we are

Pedra Silva Arquitectos is a medium-sized Lisbon practice founded in 2003 by Luis. Luis was born in South Africa to Portuguese parents and schooled in an Anglo-Saxon system before studying architecture in Lisbon. That dual background has been our edge from the start. We are a local office with international experience: we serve Portuguese clients, and we help foreign clients investing here understand Portugal's culture, bureaucracy and red tape.

We work in four areas, two established and two growing.

| Area | Position | Proof |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Workspace | Established | Google, OLX, Adidas (800 people); currently BCG, and MasterCard with Irish partners Mola Architects |
| Premium healthcare | Established | Brighton dental clinic (UK best dental clinic two years running); Sydney clinic (best in Asia-Pacific); Clínica Mulher, Lisbon |
| Residential | Growing | Three exceptional homes under construction, including an ideas-competition win for a tech CEO and a passive house overlooking the Tagus mouth |
| Hospitality | Growing | Aethos Ericeira |

For the established areas we say: look at our track record. For the growing areas we say: look at what we do when trusted, and trust us with yours.

## Positioning

**Creativity earns attention; trust earns the signature.** Pedra Silva is the firm for the exceptional client. We win them not by shouting about creativity, but by being the honest, collaborative, reliable partner in an industry full of overpromisers. The work looks extraordinary; the relationship feels safe.

We are creative without being ego-driven. Clients describe us as the opposite of prima donnas: we listen, we build their vision with them, and senior people stay hands-on. Contractors trust our drawings, because a Pedra Silva project is accurate, well designed and doesn't create problems on site.

We are honest from day one. We tell clients early what their project will really cost, how long it will really take, and what the council process involves. This sometimes loses us work to firms that overpromise. We treat that as our strongest story, not a weakness: every client burned by an overpromiser is a future client.

So the marketing leads with trust and lets the images carry the creativity.

## Audience

**One audience: anyone building something exceptional in Portugal.** We define our audience by project location and ambition, not by nationality. A Lisbon family and a foreign fund are the same target if the project is in Portugal and they want something singular.

Our best work always comes from the same kind of client: financially capable, visionary, and wanting something exceptional rather than cheap. Often they push us further than we would go ourselves. They appear in four costumes:

- **Workspace:** the corporate lead who needs a creative, bespoke office delivered on time, by people who stay close. Sleeves rolled up and a familiar relationship: the people you meet are the people who do the work, where a large firm hands the project down to whichever team is free.
- **Healthcare:** clinic founders who want a premium space, not a refurbishment, and who say "nothing is over the top" (Clínica Mulher, Sydney).
- **Residential:** the tech founder or executive building a demanding, high-quality home (the ideas-competition house, the passive house on the Tagus).
- **Hospitality:** the owner with a site or building and a vision, not a hotel chain with a design manual.

They find us in three ways, and each needs feeding differently.

| Route | Example | What it needs from us |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Online search | Clínica Mulher searched for architects of health spaces | Findable, well-photographed project pages, including in Portuguese |
| Peer referral | An architect recommended us for the ideas competition | Keep peers seeing impressive, buildable work |
| Past relationship | The Tagus client met us pitching for his company's office | Stay warm with people who have seen how we work |

We want to repel the price-shopper who picks the lowest, most optimistic quote. Honest content does this for us.

The personas below are first approximations modelled on real clients. Their fears are hypotheses, to be tested in client interviews.

## Strategic goal: become visible in Portugal

**We never left Portugal; our work just couldn't be seen.** For years much of our best work was international or confidential. Google paid the bills but could not be named or shown, so the firm had little public exposure. We are now rebuilding awareness of our work.

Portugal is the growth focus. The economy is strong, growing and stable, doing better than much of central Europe. Tourism keeps growing, which is why hospitality is our main growth bet.

International work stays as relationships we keep alive, not a market we chase: a Dublin project is nearing completion, and Russia is dormant under sanctions. Brighton (2008) and Sydney (2015) prove our level; they are not the story.

The message: a local office with international experience. We came back stronger without ever leaving.

Success in three years means a published portfolio that shows we are relevant. Proposed targets, still to be confirmed:

- 12 projects fully published online, with photos and a story
- At least 2 new hospitality commissions
- Most new work located in Portugal

## Voice and language

**We are the honest expert.** We are willing to say publicly what other architects won't say before a project starts: what it really costs, how long council approval really takes, and the mistakes clients make. This positions us as trustworthy in a field of optimistic salespeople, attracts mature clients and filters out price-shoppers.

Language:

- **Social posts in English.** Our Portuguese clients speak good English, and one post reaches both local and international audiences.
- **Portuguese for search-facing content.** Website project pages and long-form articles also exist in Portuguese, so local searches still find us.
- **Portuguese for local stories** where it is simply more natural, such as a Lisbon planning change.

Detailed tone-of-voice rules are still to be written.

## Publishing rules

**No post may break a confidentiality agreement.**

- Confidential clients are described, never named: "an 800-person office for a global technology company".
- Until confirmed client by client, treat every workspace client as describe-only. Client names in this document are for internal reference.
- Process and lessons can be shared without naming a client.
- Russian projects stay out of public marketing while the war and sanctions continue.
- Nothing is posted without publishing clearance. Ask clients about publication and photography before handover, starting with Dublin.

## Podcast direction

**One show, one audience: prospective clients and investors building in Portugal.** It is an investor's guide to building in Portugal, not a show about Pedra Silva; the authority comes from the conversations, not a sales pitch.

Every guest must pass one test: does this conversation help the listener make a better decision about building in Portugal?

- **Passes:** clients who have built here and what surprised them; investors on what they look for; council architects and planners explaining what really slows a licence. Industry guests are translators for the investor, not the audience.
- **Fails:** policy debate and industry shop talk. A civic or industry show would be a separate series with its own goal, not a drift inside this one.

**Hosts:** Luis, with Lucas, an American lawyer living between Portugal and Washington, who asks the outsider's honest, sometimes naive questions. Recorded in the firm's own studio; episodes also feed the marketing machine as clips, quotes and posts.$md$,
